ment for 4 tests? The tests are simple. Maybe I should keep it simpler.\n\nLet me reconsider. The duplication of `return PROCESSING_FEE;` is the main issue. I can address it minimally:\n\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  const basePremium = items.length === 1 ? ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] : undefined;\n\n  if (basePremium === undefined) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const firstInsurance = Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE);\n  return basePremium + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\n\nThis eliminates the explicit `items.length !== 1` conditional by folding it into the ternary. The duplicated `return PROCESSING_FEE;` is gone.\n\nBut this changes the semantics slightly: originally, `ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type]` could be undefined for a known item type if the lookup returns undefined (which it does for unknown keys). The ternary guards against `items[0]` being undefined when length is 0.\n\nMass: similar to original.\n\nClarity: the ternary is less clear than two separate ifs, in my opinion. But the removal of duplicated return is good.\n\nHmm. Let me think of yet another option.\n\nWhat about renaming `calculateQuotePremium` to reflect that it's only for single items, and moving the guard out?\n\n```typescript\nfunction calculateSingleItemQuotePremium(item: Item): number {\n  const basePremium = ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type];\n  const firstInsurance = Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE);\n  return basePremium + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  if (items.length !== 1) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n  return calculateSingleItemQuotePremium(items[0]);\n}\n```\n\nThis is worse - more functions, and `calculateSingleItemQuotePremium` assumes the item type exists.\n\nOK, let me step back. I think the best minimal refactor is to improve naming in `processScenario` and reduce the fallback duplication in `calculateQuotePremium`.\n\nActually, I just realized something. The `processScenario` function returns a `Results` object, and for claim steps it returns `{ results: [{ premium: PROCESSING_FEE }] }` which is typed as `Results` but semantically wrong. When claim tests are added, this will change. But for now, it's dead code for claim tests.\n\nMaybe I can refactor `processScenario` to be clearer about handling only quotes currently:\n\n```typescript\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): Results {\n  const step = scenario.steps[0];\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items) }] };\n  }\n  return { results: [{ premium: PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n}\n```\n\nThis is already pretty simple. The claim fallback is weird but minimal.\n\nWhat if I name the claim fallback explicitly?\n\n```typescript\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): Results {\n  const step = scenario.steps[0];\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items) }] };\n  }\n  return claimFallbackResult();\n}\n\nfunction claimFallbackResult(): Results {\n  return { results: [{ premium: PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n}\n```\n\nNo, that's worse - adding a function for dead code.\n\nI think I'll go with extracting `premiumWithFee` and combining the guard conditions. This addresses both duplication and prepares for future extension.\n\nWait, but the instructions say \"Make ONE improvement at a time\" and \"Run tests after each change\". I should do one refactoring, run tests, then consider another. But as a subagent, I can do multiple if they all make sense.\n\nLet me start with the most obvious: extract the premium formula and combine the guard conditions.\n\nActually, let me reconsider combining the guard conditions. With the ternary, I access `items[0].type` only when `items.length === 1`, so it's safe. This is a clean refactor.\n\nFinal plan:\n1. Extract `premiumWithFee(basePremium)` function\n2. In `calculateQuotePremium`, combine the two guard conditions using a ternary to eliminate duplicated `return PROCESSING_FEE;`\n\nLet me write the refactored code:\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM = 100;\nconst AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst STAFF_BASE_PREMIUM = 80;\n\nconst ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM,\n  amulet: AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM,\n  staff: STAFF_BASE_PREMIUM,\n};\n\nfunction premiumWithFee(basePremium: number): number {\n  const firstInsurance = Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE);\n  return basePremium + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  const basePremium = items.length === 1 ? ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] : undefined;\n\n  if (basePremium === undefined) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  return premiumWithFee(basePremium);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, is `premiumWithFee` a good name? It calculates base + first insurance + processing fee. Maybe `calculatePremiumWithFee` is better? Or `totalPremium`?\n\nActually, looking at the test names, they say \"100 base + 10 first insurance + 5 fee\". So the total is base + first insurance + fee. A name like `totalPremium` might be clearer.\n\nBut `totalPremium` could imply summing multiple items. `premiumIncludingFee`? `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee`?\n\nI'll go with `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee` for clarity, or maybe `calculatePremiumWithInsuranceAndFee`. But the `calculate` prefix is redundant if context is clear.\n\nWait, the function `calculateQuotePremium` already uses `calculate`. Let me keep naming consistent but not redundant. I'll use `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee`.\n\nActually, I realize `premiumWithFee` might be misinterpreted as just adding the fee. `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee` is clearer but longer. Given Rule 2, clarity wins. I'll use `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee`.\n\nWait, there's another consideration. The spec has lots of modifiers (curse, enchantment, material, loyalty, follow-up). The base premium + first insurance + fee is the core formula. Future code will likely calculate premiums for individual items and then sum. So this function represents the premium for a single base premium amount.\n\nMaybe `singleItemPremium`? No, that's not quite right either.\n\nI'll go with `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee`.\n\nNow, about the ternary guard. Is it clear? Let me write:\n\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  const basePremium = items.length === 1 ? ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] : undefined;\n\n  if (basePremium === undefined) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  return premiumWithInsuranceAndFee(basePremium);\n}\n```\n\nThis reads: \"If there's exactly one item, look up its base premium; otherwise undefined. If we didn't find a base premium, return just the processing fee. Otherwise, calculate the full premium.\"\n\nThis is clear enough. The ternary is a bit dense but acceptable.\n\nActually, let me think if there's a way to avoid the ternary and still remove duplication. What if I use short-circuiting?\n\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  if (items.length !== 1) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const basePremium = ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type];\n  if (basePremium === undefined) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  return premiumWithInsuranceAndFee(basePremium);\n}\n```\n\nThis still has duplicated `return PROCESSING_FEE;`. But it's very clear. Maybe clarity is better than removing the duplication.\n\nHmm. The duplication is conceptual: both cases mean \"we can't calculate a real premium, so return just the fee\". I could express this with a single check if I think of it as \"valid base premium\".\n\nTernary version:\n```typescript\nconst basePremium = items.length === 1 ? ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] : undefined;\n```\n\nThis creates `basePremium` which is either a valid base premium or undefined. Then one check.\n\nI think the ternary version is better for DRY. Let me go with it.\n\nNow, should I also consider changing `processScenario`? With only quote tests, it's fine. The claim fallback will need changing later. I'll leave it for now.\n\nLet me also double-check: the current `processScenario` always returns `{ results: [{ premium: ... }] }`, even for claims. This will break type checking once claim tests exist. But for now, tests pass. I won't touch it.\n\nActually wait, I should check if TypeScript has any issues with the current code. The return type for claim is wrong type-wise (`premium` instead of `payout`/`remainingCap`). But TypeScript's structural typing allows excess properties, and since `Result` is `QuoteResult | ClaimResult`, returning `{ premium: ... }` is valid as `QuoteResult`. So type checking passes.\n\nOK let me implement the refactoring. I'll write the file and run tests.\n\nWait, I should also consider whether to extract `premiumWithInsuranceAndFee` or not. Let me think about the mass change and clarity.\n\nWithout extraction (just ternary guard):\nOriginal function mass: ~29\nNew function mass:\n- Constants: 1, undefined = 2\n- Bindings: items, basePremium, firstInsurance, ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS, PROCESSING_FEE, FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE, Math = 7\n- Invocations: Math.ceil = 1\n- Conditionals: 1 ternary + 1 if = 8\n- Assignments: 2 const = 12\nTotal: 30 (slightly more due to ternary vs if? Actually same conditionals mass, so same)\n\nWait, original had 2 ifs = 8 conditionals. New has 1 ternary + 1 if = 8 conditionals. So same.\n\nMass is essentially the same. But clarity: removed duplicated return, but added ternary. Net clarity maybe slightly better due to less duplication.\n\nWith extraction:\nFunction mass decreases to ~22, plus new function mass ~12 = ~34 total. Mass increases by ~5.\n\nClarity improves by separating \"calculate full premium\" formula from \"determine if quote is calculable\".\n\nGiven Rule 2 trumps APP, extraction is a