th.ceil counts as invocation. `calculatePremiumWithInsuranceAndFee` count. `calculateQuotePremium` count. `+` operators count.\n\nLet's define mass counting:\n- Constants: numeric literals and string literals (including property keys in object literal). Exclude type annotations.\n- Bindings: every `const`/`let`/function declaration/parameter/local variable.\n- Invocations: function calls and binary operators (arithmetic, comparison, logical, `??`, `===`, etc.) and unary? Hmm. To keep simple, count function calls and arithmetic/comparison operators. But maybe overcount. The example only counts `+`. It didn't count `=>` or `.`. We can follow: count function calls and infix operators (`+`, `*`, `===`, `??`, etc.). Maybe property access `.` not counted. Array index `[]` not counted.\n- Conditionals: `if` and ternary `?:`.\n- Loops: none; reduce/map count as loops but we don't have.\n- Assignments: reassignments only, not const init.\n\nThis is a bit arbitrary but transparent. We can also simplify mass count to main functions only and explain. The report can include component count.\n\nLet's compute current mass with these rules.\n\nCurrent code (executable, ignoring type interfaces):\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM = 100;\nconst AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst STAFF_BASE_PREMIUM = 80;\nconst POTION_BASE_PREMIUM = 40;\n\nconst ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM,\n  amulet: AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM,\n  staff: STAFF_BASE_PREMIUM,\n  potion: POTION_BASE_PREMIUM,\n};\n\nfunction calculatePremiumWithInsuranceAndFee(basePremium: number): number {\n  const firstInsurance = Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE);\n  return basePremium + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  const basePremium = items.length === 1 ? ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] : undefined;\n\n  if (basePremium === undefined) {\n    return PROCESS