` = 1\n`const COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM = 25;` - constant `25` = 1\n\nObject literal keys: \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\", \"rune\", \"moonstone\" = 6 constants\n\nTotal constants: 7 + 6 = 13\n\nBindings:\n- `PROCESSING_FEE, FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE, SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM, AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM, STAFF_BASE_PREMIUM, POTION_BASE_PREMIUM, COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM` = 7\n- `ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS` = 1\n- In `calculatePremiumWithFirstInsuranceAndFee`: `basePremium`, `firstInsurance` = 2\n- In `calculateQuotePremium`: `items`, `basePremium` = 2\n- In `processScenario`: `scenario`, `step` = 2\n\nTotal bindings: 7 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 14\n\nInvocations:\n- `Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE)` - `Math.ceil` invocation = 1, `*` is not an invocation, it's an operator\n- `basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` - no invocation\n- `basePremium + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE` - no invocation\n- `calculateQuotePremium(step.items)` = 1\n- `calculatePremiumWithFirstInsuranceAndFee(basePremium)` = 1\n- `ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[items[0].type]` - no invocation\n- `items.length === 1` - no invocation\n- `step.op === \"quote\"` - no invocation\n\nWait, do we count property access as invocation? Probably not. Method calls count.\n\nInvocations: Math.ceil(1), calculateQuotePremium(1), calculatePremiumWithFirstInsuranceAndFee(1) = 3\n\nConditionals:\n- `basePremium === undefined ? PROCESSING_FEE : calculatePremiumWithFirstInsuranceAndFee(basePremium)` = 1 ternary\n- `if (step.op === \"quote\")` = 1\n\nTotal conditionals: 2\n\nLoops: 0\n\nAssignments:\n- `const` declarations are bindings with assignments. But assignment count in APP usually refers to mutations/reassignments, not initial declarations. Let me check the guidelines: \"Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\"\n- `const` declarations are not counted as assignments? The examples show `x = 5` and `count++` as mutations. But bindings count includes variables and parameters. So const declarations are bindings (1 mass each),