operator might be counted as conditional because it branches. Let me think. The APP rules list conditionals as `if`, `switch`, `?:`. Nullish coalescing (`??`) and logical OR (`||`) are shorthand conditionals. The example doesn't cover these. I think for consistency, `??` should count as a conditional because it evaluates the right side only if left is null/undefined. But it's also a binary operator. I'll count it as a conditional.\n\ncalculateFinalPremium:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateFinalPremium(basePremium: number, itemModifiers: number): number {\n  const firstInsurance = Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE);\n  return basePremium + itemModifiers + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\nInvocations:\n- Math.ceil (1)\n- basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE (multiplication)\n- basePremium + itemModifiers (addition)\n- ... + firstInsurance (addition)\n- ... + PROCESSING_FEE (addition)\nTotal: 5 invocations\n\ncalculateQuotePremium:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const basePremium = items.every(isComponentItem)\n    ? calculateComponentBlockBasePremium(items)\n    : calculateStandardBasePremium(items);\n\n  if (basePremium === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const itemModifiers = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + calculateItemModifier(item), 0);\n  return calculateFinalPremium(basePremium, itemModifiers);\n}\n```\nInvocations:\n- items.length (property access - not invocation? length is property access, usually not counted as invocation. But in some APP interpretations, `.` access is an invocation. The example didn't count property access as invocation. I'll not count property access.)\n- items.every (1)\n- isComponentItem (1) - actually `items.every(isComponentItem)` passes isComponentItem as callback, so it's one invocation of every with isComponentItem. The isComponentItem itself is invoked internally by every. But do we count every internal invocation? APP usually counts explicit invocations. So `every` is 1, and we don't separately count the callback invocations inside.\n- calculateComponentBlockBasePremium (1)\n- calculateStandardBasePremium (1)\n- items.reduce (1)\n- calculateItemModifier (1)\n- sum + calculateItemModifier(item) (addition)\n- calculateFinalPremium (1)\nTotal: 7 invocations\n\nprocessScenario:\n```typescript\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): Results {\n  const step = scenario.steps[0];\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items) }] };\n  }\n  return { results: [{ premium: PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n}\n```\nInvocations:\n- scenario.steps[0] (property access + indexing - not counted as invocation)\n- calculateQuotePremium (1)\nTotal: 1 invocation\n\nTotal invocations across all functions: 7 + 3 + 5 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 1 = wait I need to recount.\n\nLet me recount invocations per function:\n1. calculateFinalPremium: Math.ceil, * , +, +, + = 5\n2. calculateItemModifier: basePremiumForType, *, ??, *, += (not) = let's say 4 (if * counts) or fewer. Actually `modifier += basePremium * 0.5` contains one multiplication and one +=. The += is assignment. So invocations: basePremiumForType(1) + *(1) + ??(1) + *(1) = 4\n3. basePremiumForType: 0\n4. isComponentItem: basePremiumForType = 1\n5. calculateComponentBlockBasePremium: Set(1) + map(1) + reduce(1) + filter(1) + basePremiumForType(1) + *(1) + +(1) = 7\n6. calculateStandardBasePremium: reduce(1) + basePremiumForType(1) + +(1) = 3\n7. calculateQuotePremium: items.length(not) + every(1) + calculateComponentBlockBasePremium(1) + calculateStandardBasePremium(1) + reduce(1) + calculateItemModifier(1) + +(1) + calculateFinalPremium(1) = 7\n8. processScenario: calculateQuotePremium(1) = 1\n\nTotal invocations: 5 + 4 + 0 + 1 + 7 + 3 + 7 + 1 = 28\n\nConditionals:\n- if (item.cursed) in calculateItemModifier: 1\n- if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 5) in calculateItemModifier: 1\n