7\n\nSo total mass = constants(21) + bindings(29) + invocations(28) + conditionals(8) + loops(6) + assignments(17) = 109\n\nHmm, this is a rough estimate. Different counting methods yield different numbers. The key is to compare before/after consistently.\n\nNow, what refactoring should I apply? Let me think about the Four Rules:\n\n1. Tests pass - yes.\n2. Reveals intent - names are OK, but magic numbers (0.5, 0.3, 0.1) could be named constants to reveal intent.\n3. No duplication - There's duplication of early return PROCESSING_FEE in calculateQuotePremium. Also, the logic checking for \"no premium\" appears twice.\n4. Fewest elements - Maybe some functions could be simplified.\n\n**Refactoring ideas**:\n\nA. Name the modifier rates:\n   - `const CURSE_MODIFIER_RATE = 0.5;`\n   - `const HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_MODIFIER_RATE = 0.3;`\n   - `const HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;`\n   This reveals intent (Rule 2).\n\nB. Consolidate the two `return PROCESSING_FEE` early returns in calculateQuotePremium. But careful: the first checks empty items, the second checks zero base premium. We could restructure:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const basePremium = items.every(isComponentItem)\n    ? calculateComponentBlockBasePremium(items)\n    : calculateStandardBasePremium(items);\n\n  if (basePremium === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const itemModifiers = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + calculateItemModifier(item), 0);\n  return calculateFinalPremium(basePremium, itemModifiers);\n}\n```\nThe two returns both return PROCESSING_FEE. They could be combined after calculating basePremium. But the empty case needs special handling because `items.every(isComponentItem)` on empty array returns true, and `calculateComponentBlockBasePremium([])` returns 0, so we'd hit the second check. We could remove the first if:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  const basePremium = items.every(isComponentItem)\n    ? calculateComponentBlockBasePremium(items)\n    : calculateStandardBasePremium(items);\n\n  if (basePremium === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n\n  const itemModifiers = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + calculateItemModifier(item), 0);\n  return calculateFinalPremium(basePremium, itemModifiers);\n}\n```\nThis removes one conditional and one constant (the 0 in `items.length === 0`). But does it keep tests green? Let me check:\n- Empty items: `items.every(isComponentItem)` returns true for empty array. `calculateComponentBlockBasePremium([])`: `types = [...new Set([])]` = [], `types.reduce(..., 0)` = 0. So basePremium = 0, returns PROCESSING_FEE = 5. Good.\n- Single sword: `items.every(isComponentItem)` = false (sword base premium 100 != 25). `calculateStandardBasePremium([sword])` = 100. basePremium != 0. itemModifiers = 0. calculateFinalPremium(100, 0) = 100 + 0 + 10 + 5 = 115. Good.\n- 3 runes: `items.every(isComponentItem)` = true (all 25). calculateComponentBlockBasePremium returns 60. Good.\n- Unknown type: basePremium = 0, returns PROCESSING_FEE. Good.\n\nThis refactoring removes duplication of the `return PROCESSING_FEE` concept? Actually it removes one early return but keeps the logic. It reduces mass by 1 conditional + 1 constant. But wait, it also removes a binding (`items` was already bound), and one `if` conditional. It keeps the code clearer by having one place that decides \"only fee\".\n\nBut is it clearer? The first `if (items.length === 0)` explicitly states the empty case, which is more intent-revealing. Removing it makes the code rely on the fact that empty arrays produce basePremium 0. This might be less clear (Rule 2). However, the comment in the test says \"only the processing fee\", and the second if with `basePremium === 0` also captures \"no chargeable premium\". \n\nHmm, Rule 2 trumps APP. So if removing the explicit empty check reduces clarity, we shouldn't do 