+ +(1) = 3\nTotal: 35\n\nSlightly better. But wait, the formula `return -basePremium * (loyalty + followUp)` means discount = basePremium × (loyaltyRate + followUpRate). That's correct and cleaner.\n\nHmm but readability: `-basePremium * (loyalty + followUp)` could be confusing. Better to use:\n```ts\nreturn basePremium * -(loyalty + followUp);\n```\nOr:\n```ts\nreturn -1 * basePremium * (loyalty + followUp);\n```\n\nActually `return -(basePremium * (loyalty + followUp))` is clearest.\n\nMass: -(1) + *(1) + *(1) + +(1) = 4? Wait:\n- `basePremium * (loyalty + followUp)` is one `*` with `+` inside\n- `-(...)` is unary minus invocation\nTotal: 1 (unary -) + 1 (*) + 1 (+) = 3.\n\nSo total: 16 + 16 + 3 = 35.\n\n### Option 3: Simplify calculateItemModifier\nCurrent:\n```ts\nfunction calculateItemModifier(item: Item): number {\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForType(item.type);\n  const curseSurcharge = item.cursed\n    ? basePremium * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE\n    : 0;\n  const highEnchantmentSurcharge = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n    ? basePremium * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n    : 0;\n  return curseSurcharge + highEnchantmentSurcharge;\n}\n```\n\nCould be:\n```ts\nfunction calculateItemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForType(item.type);\n  const curse = item.cursed ? CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantment = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  return basePremium * (curse + enchantment);\n}\n```\n\nOld mass: 45\nNew mass:\n- binding item: 1\n- binding basePremium: 1\n- assign: 6\n- invocation basePremiumForType: 1\n- binding curse: 1\n- assign: 6\n- ?:(4) + cursed(0) + const ref(0) + 0(1) = 5? Wait `item.cursed` is property access. Not counting. `?:` is 4. `CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE` is const ref (0). `0` is const (1). So: 6 + 4 + 1 = 11 for the assignment expression.\n- binding enchantment: 1\n- assign: 6\n- `item.enchantment ?? 0`: ??(4) + en