n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nCould use `reduce` to eliminate mutable `quoteCount` and `results`, but that would increase mass and reduce clarity. The current imperative loop is fine for this case.\n\nActually we can extract the step processing:\n\n```ts\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): Results {\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  const results = scenario.steps.map((step) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const premium = calculateQuotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, quoteCount);\n      quoteCount += 1;\n      return { premium };\n    }\n    return { payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 };\n  });\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nBut `map` with side effect on quoteCount is confusing. Better to keep for loop.\n\n### Option 6: Simplify countItemsByType\nUsing a loop instead of reduce could reduce mass:\n\n```ts\nfunction countItemsByType(items: Item[]): Record<string, number> {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts[item.type] = (counts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n```\n\nOld mass: 25\nNew mass:\n- binding items: 1\n- binding counts: 1\n- assignment = {}: 6 + object literal(1) = 7\n- loop for: 5\n- binding item: 1\n- assignment counts[...] = ...: 6\n- computed property access counts[item.type] LHS: 1\n- computed property access counts[item.type] RHS: 1\n- ??(4) + 0(1) = 5\n- +(1)\n- constant 1(1)\nTotal: 1+1+7+5+1+6+1+1+5+1+1 = 30\n\nThat's higher! So reduce is better for mass here.\n\n### Option 7: Rename `calculateItemModifier` and `calculatePolicyModifiers`\nThis is the easiest and improves Rule 2. Names should reveal intent.\n\n## Decided Refactorings\n\nI'll do these refactorings:\n1. Rename `calculateItemModifier` to `calculateItemSurcharges` (better intent)\n2. Rename `calculatePolicyModifiers` to `calculatePolicyDiscounts` (better intent)\n3. Simplify `calculatePolicyDiscounts` to declarative style with rates\n4. Simplify `calculateItemSurcharges` to compu