n\" so count as loop.\n\nOK let me redo.\n\nActually, I'll just count reasonably and document.\n\nCurrent code analysis for duplication and simplification:\n\n1. In `calculateDamageReimbursement`:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateDamageReimbursement(damage: Damage, item: Item): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD) {\n    return damage.amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  if (item.material === DRAGON_MATERIAL) {\n    return damage.amount;\n  }\n  return damage.amount;\n}\n```\nThe last two branches both return `damage.amount`. This is duplication! We can simplify to:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateDamageReimbursement(damage: Damage, item: Item): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD) {\n    return damage.amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  return damage.amount;\n}\n```\nThis removes a conditional and duplication. Tests should still pass.\n\n2. In `calculateClaimResult`:\n```typescript\nconst reimbursement = item !== undefined\n  ? calculateDamageReimbursement(damage, item)\n  : damage.amount;\n```\nThe ternary conditional here. If item is undefined, just use damage.amount. Otherwise call function. We could extract a helper but it might not help. Actually, since `calculateDamageReimbursement` already returns damage.amount for non-high-enchantment items, we could just check for item existence.\n\nBut wait, the function already handles this. The ternary is fine but maybe could be clearer. We could change to:\n```typescript\nconst reimbursement = item === undefined\n  ? damage.amount\n  : calculateDamageReimbursement(damage, item);\n```\nThis is just reordering, no mass change.\n\n3. In `calculateQuotePremium`:\n```typescript\nconst basePremium = items.every(isComponentItem)\n  ? calculateComponentBlockBasePremium(items)\n  : calculateStandardBasePremium(items);\n\nif (basePremium === 0) {\n  return PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\nThis handles empty item list. But `calculateStandardBasePremium([])` returns 0, and `calculateComponentBlockBasePremium([])` also returns 0. The check is needed. But maybe the name or logic could be clearer.\n\nActually, we could move the empty check earlier. But not a big improvement.\n\n4. `calculatePolicyDiscounts` returns a negative number. The name is \"calculate discounts\" but it returns negative modifiers. Could be renamed `calculatePolicyModifier` or keep as is with comment. Not critical.\n\n5. Duplication in `calculateTotalPremium` - it adds all the modifiers. Fine.\n\n6. The `calculateItemSurcharge` has two similar checks for rate. Could be refactored but might hurt clarity.\n\n7. Big potential refactor: The constants are scattered. They could be grouped better by domain (quote vs claim). This would improve intent.\n\n8. Another refactor: Extract `effectiveEnchantment(item)` helper to avoid `(item.enchantment ?? 0)` duplication. It's used in `calculateItemSurcharge` and `calculateDamageReimbursement`.\n\nLet me check: \n- `calculateItemSurcharge`: `(item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD`\n- `calculateDamageReimbursement`: `(item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD`\n\nYes, the `(item.enchantment ?? 0)` pattern is duplicated. Extracting `effectiveEnchantment(item)` would reduce duplication. This is a good refactor.\n\n9. Another refactor: `DRAGON_MATERIAL` constant is only used once in the simplified code (after removing the duplicate branch), so it could be inlined. But inlining might hurt intent. Better to keep the constant for clarity.\n\nActually after simplification of `calculateDamageReimbursement`, `DRAGON_MATERIAL` is no longer used at all! So we should remove it (Fewest Elements). But wait - the test \"should pay out 700 G for dragon-material sword enchantment 5 damage 800\" is currently todo. The constant might be needed for future tests. However, the instructions say refactor based on current passing tests. Since no current test uses dragon mat