oking at `processScenario`:\n\n```typescript\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): Results {\n  const policyStates: PolicyState[] = [];\n  const results: Result[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const quoteIndex = policyStates.length;\n      policyStates.push({\n        policy: step,\n        remainingCap: calculatePolicyCap(step.items),\n      });\n      results.push({ premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, quoteIndex) });\n    } else {\n      const state = policyStates[step.policy];\n      const result = calculateClaimResult(step, state.policy, state.remainingCap);\n      state.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThere's some potential duplication in `policyStates.push` vs the claim side. But not obvious.\n\nOne concrete duplication: The quote branch calls `calculatePolicyCap(step.items)` which is `calculateInsuranceSum(step.items) * POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER`. And `calculateQuotePremium` internally calls `calculateBasePremium(step.items)`. Both traverse items. But extracting common logic would be complex and not necessarily clearer.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe I can remove a conditional by using a default value pattern. For example, in `calculateItemSurcharge`:\n\n```typescript\nconst curseRate = item.cursed ? CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\nconst enchantmentRate = effectiveEnchantment(item) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n  ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n  : 0;\n```\n\nCould be:\n```typescript\nfunction curseRate(item: Item): number {\n  return item.cursed ? CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n}\nfunction enchantmentRate(item: Item): number {\n  return effectiveEnchantment(item) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n}\n```\n\nBut that adds functions. Not necessarily better.\n\nOr could use a small helper:\n```typescript\nfunction rateIf(condition: boolean, rate: number): number {