nCombined: 23 + 9 = 32. Down from 43. But this adds a function. Clarity? The helper name `calculateDamagePayout` reveals intent. But using `findPolicyItem` inside reduce with `!` is a bit dense.\n\nActually, the `!` assertion is risky. If `findPolicyItem` returns undefined, the error occurs at runtime. But `validateClaim` already ensures the item exists, so `!` is safe. However, if we inline the lookup into reduce, it's less clear.\n\nMaybe keep the for loop but extract `calculateDamagePayout`:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateDamagePayout(damage: Damage, item: Item): number {\n  const reimbursableDamage = calculateReimbursableDamage(damage, item);\n  return Math.max(0, reimbursableDamage - DEDUCTIBLE);\n}\n\nfunction calculateClaimResult(...) {\n  validateClaim(...);\n  let totalPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of claim.incident.damages) {\n    const item = findPolicyItem(policy.items, damage.itemType)!;\n    totalPayout += calculateDamagePayout(damage, item);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalPayout, remainingCap));\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nMass:\n- calculateDamagePayout: 3 bindings + 3 invocations = 9\n- calculateClaimResult: 8 bindings + validateClaim(1) + findPolicyItem(1) + calculateDamagePayout(1) + += (1) + Math.min(1) + Math.floor(1) + -(1) = 8 + 7 = 15? Plus loop 5, assignments 12. Total = 32.\n- Current mass: ~43\n- Improvement: ~11 mass units\n\nThis is good. It improves clarity and reduces mass. But it adds a function element. Rule 4 says minimize elements, but Rule 2 and 3 come first. The helper reveals intent and reduces duplication (of the deductible subtraction logic, though it's only used once, the extraction makes the claim result calculation clearer).\n\nWait, the deductible logic is only used once, so extracting it doesn't reduce duplication. It just improves clarity by naming the concept. That's a valid Rule 2 improvement.\n\nBut adding a function is an element. Is it worth it? I think