ations: validateQuoteItems (1), countItemsByType (1), every (1), isComponentItem (1), Object.entries (1), reduce (1), calculateTypePremium (1), + (1) = 8\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 1\n- Assignments: 0\nTotal: 1 + 6 + 16 + 5 = 28\n\nCombined: 11 + 28 = 39. Slightly higher than 38. Not worth mass-wise, but could improve clarity by naming the component block concept.\n\nIf I also rename `allComponents` to `onlyComponentItems`, clarity improves further.\n\nBut again, mass increases slightly. Rule 2 may justify it.\n\nActually, I think the most valuable refactorings are naming improvements. Let me focus on those since they don't risk mass increases much and clearly improve Rule 2.\n\nLet me also consider `calculateTotalPremium`. It computes the final premium. The name is okay. But looking at the function:\n\n```typescript\nfunction calculateTotalPremium(\n  basePremium: number,\n  itemModifiers: number,\n  policyDiscount: number,\n): number {\n  const firstInsurance = Math.ceil(basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE);\n  return basePremium + itemModifiers + firstInsurance - policyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\n\nThe parameter `policyDiscount` is a positive number representing the discount amount. The function subtracts it. Maybe rename parameter to `discount`? Or keep `policyDiscount`. It's fine.\n\nWhat about renaming `calculateTotalPremium` to `calculateFinalPremium`? \"Final\" might better indicate it's the last step. But \"Total\" is also good.\n\nActually, I just noticed: `calculateTotalPremium` does NOT apply ceiling to the total, only to the first insurance. The final premium can be a non-integer? But tests expect integers. Let's check: for rune single, base=25, firstInsurance=ceil(2.5)=3, total=25+0+0-0+5=33. Good. For 7 runes: base=175, firstInsurance=ceil(17.5)=18, total=175+0+18+5=198. Good.\n\nThe name `calculateTotalPremium` is fine.\n\nLet me look at the constants. They are well-named. `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` is a bit unusual - \"first insurance\" is a domain term. OK.\n\nI think the refactorings I'll do are:\n1. Rename `calculateDamageReimbursement` → `calculateReimbursableDamage` (Rule 2)\n2. Rename local variable `reimbursement` → `reimbursableDamage` in `calculateClaimResult` (Rule 2)\n3. Rename `allComponents` → `onlyComponentItems` in `calculateBasePremium` (Rule 2)\n\nThese are pure naming improvements. Mass stays the same. They improve clarity.\n\nBut the task says I should calculate APP mass before and after. If mass stays the same, I should document that. That's fine.\n\nWait, should I also try to reduce mass? Let me see if there's a way to reduce mass without hurting clarity.\n\nIn `validateClaim`:\n```typescript\nfunction validateClaim(claim: ClaimStep, policy: QuoteStep): void {\n  for (const damage of claim.incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(\"Damage amount cannot be negative\");\n    }\n  }\n  const damageCounts = countBy(claim.incident.damages, (damage) => damage.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countItemsByType(policy.items);\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    if (count > (policyCounts[type] ?? 0)) {\n      throw new Error(`Claim damages more ${type} items than policy covers`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCould use `some` instead of `for...of` for negative check:\n```typescript\nif (claim.incident.damages.some((damage) => damage.amount < 0)) {\n  throw new Error(\"Damage amount cannot be negative\");\n}\n```\n\nMass: current has loop (5) and conditional (4) = 9 for that part. New has invocation some (2), conditional if (4), invocation < (2) = 8. Slight mass reduction. But is `some` clearer? It's a common pattern. Maybe slightly more idiomatic. But the for...of is also clear. I think this is a minor improvement.\n\nBut wait, if I change to `some`, I lose the ability to know which damage is negative. But the error message is generic anyway.\n\nActually, I think the for...of is fine and explicit. The some version is concise but maybe less clear for beginners. I'll skip.\n\nWhat about `calculatePolicyDiscount`:\n```typescript\nconst loyaltyRate = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\nconst followUpRate = quoteIndex > 0 ? FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n```\n\nCould extract helpers:\n```typescript\nfunction loyaltyDiscountRate(customer: Customer): number {\n  return customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n}\nfunction followUpDiscountRate(quoteIndex: number): number {\n  return quoteIndex > 0 ? FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis adds elements. Mass increases. Not worth it.\n\nOK, I think I'll stick with the naming improvements. Let me also check the file structure - maybe I can group related functions better? The constants are already grouped. The functions are in a reasonable order.\n\nOne more idea: The `validateQuoteItems` function:\n```typescript\nfunction validateQuoteItems(items: Item[]): void {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] === undefined) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCould use `find` or `some`:\n```typescript\nfunction validateQuoteItems(items: Item[]): void {\n  const unknown = items.find((item) => ITEM_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] === undefined);\n  if (unknown) {\n    throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${unknown.type}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nMass: current has loop (5), conditional (4) = 9. New has find (2), conditional (4), property access, object access = 2 + 4 = 6. Mass reduction. But does it improve clarity? It changes the error message to show the first unknown type instead of the last (since find returns first). Current for...of throws on the last unknown type. Both are valid. The `find` version is more idiomatic TypeScript.\n\nBut this is changing behavior slightly (which unknown type is reported if multiple). However, tests probably only check one unknown type at a time. So tests should pass.\n\nHmm, this is a good candidate. It reduces mass and improves clarity (declarative vs imperative). But does it reveal intent better? `find` says \"find the first unknown item\". The for loop says \"iterate and throw on unknown\". Both are clear.\n\nI'll consider this. But maybe the for loop is more explicit about validation. I'll keep it simple.\n\nLet me go with the naming improvements. I'll do them all at once since they're all renames, or one at a time to be safe. The instructions say \"Make ONE improvement at a time\". But three renames are related and low-risk. I'll do them in one edit and run tests.\n\nActually, let me be more disciplined. I'll do:\n1. Rename `calculateDamageReimbursement` → `calculateReimbursableDamage` (and update variable name)\n2. Run tests\n3. Rename `allComponents` → `onlyComponentItems`\n4. Run tests\n\nThis way I follow \"one improvement at a time\" loosely.\n\nWait, the rename of the function and the variable are one conceptual change. I'll do them together.\n\nLet me start.\n\nFirst, document initial mass. I'll calculate for the affected functions.\n\nInitial mass of `calculateDamageReimbursement`: 12\nInitial mass of `calculateClaimResult`: 46\nInitial mass of `calculateBasePremium`: 38\n\nTotal affected mass: 96\n\nAfter renames only:\n- `calculateReimbursableDamage`: 12\n- `calculateClaimResult`: 46\n- `calculateBasePremium`: 38\nTotal: 96 (no mass change)\n\nAfter `allComponents` → `onlyComponentItems`: still 38 (one binding renamed).\n\nSo overall no mass change, but Rule 2 improved.\n\nIs there any refactoring I can do that reduces mass? Let me look once more.\n\nActually, I realize I could potentially reduce mass in `processScenario` by removing the `quoteIndex` variable and inlining `policyStates.length`:\n\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") {\n  policyStates.push({\n    policy: step,\n    remainingCap: calculatePolicyCap(step.items),\n  });\n  results.push({ premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, policyStates.length - 1) });\n}\n```\n\nWait, no. Currently `quoteIndex = policyStates.length` BEFORE pushing. After push, length is +1. So we need `policyStates.length - 1`. This removes one binding but adds a `-` invocation and a constant. Mass change: -1 binding, +1 invocation (+2), +1 constant (+1) = +2. Mass increases.\n\nAlternatively, push returns the new length, but we can't easily get the old index.\n\nSo not a mass win.\n\nWhat about `calculateQuotePremium`:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): number {\n  const basePremium = calculateBasePremium(items);\n  const itemModifiers = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + calculateItemSurcharge(item), 0);\n  const policyDiscount = calculatePolicyDiscount(basePremium, customer, quoteIndex);\n  return calculateTotalPremium(basePremium, itemModifiers, policyDiscount);\n}\n```\n\nCould inline all variables:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number {\n  const basePremium = calculateBasePremium(items);\n  return calculateTotalPremium(\n    basePremium,\n    items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + calculateItemSurcharge(item), 0),\n    calculatePolicyDiscount(basePremium, customer, quoteIndex),\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThis removes 2 bindings (itemModifiers, policyDiscount) but adds complexity. Mass: -2 bindings. Not worth it for clarity.\n\nOK, I'll proceed with naming improvements.\n\nWait, let me think about whether `calculateDamageReimbursement` is really a bad name. The function returns the amount that will be reimbursed, before deductible. \"Reimbursement\" can mean \"the amount to be repaid\". So it's not terrible. But `calculateReimbursableDamage` is more precise: it calculates the damage amount that is reimbursable (eligible for reimbursement). The tests support this: for enchantment >= 8, only 50% of damage is reimbursable.\n\nI think it's a good rename.\n\nLet me also think about `allComponents` → `onlyComponentItems`. This clearly indicates \"all items are component items\" rather than \"all components are included\". Good improvement.\n\nOK, let me make the edits.\n\nStep 1: Rename function and variable.\nI need to edit `src/claim-office.ts`.\n\nThe function definition:\n```typescript\nfunction calculateDamageReimbursement(damage: Damage, item: Item): number {\n```\n\nThe call site:\n```typescript\nconst reimbursement = calculateDamageReimbursement(damage, item);\n```\n\nI'll replace both.\n\nThen run tests.\n\nThen Step 2: Rename `allComponents` to `onlyComponentItems`.\n\nThen run tests.\n\nLet me do it.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Evaluation\n\n**Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY):**\n- `calculateDamageReimbursement`: The function returns the damage amount *eligible* for reimbursement (applying the 50% enchantment limit), not the actual reimbursement paid. The name is misleading.\n- `allComponents` in `calculateBasePremium`: Ambiguous — could mean \"all component types present\" rather than \"all items are components\".\n- Local `reimbursement` variable: Mirrors the misleading function name.\n\n**Current APP Mass (affected functions):**\n- `calculateDamageReimbursement`: bindings 2 + invocations 3 + conditional 1 = **12**\n- `calculateClaimResult`: constants 1 + bindings 8 + invocations 10 + loop 1 + assignments 2 = **46**\n- `calculateBasePremium`: constants 1 + bindings 6 + invocations 11 + conditional 1 + loop 1 = **38**\n\n**Total affected mass: 96**\n\nI'll apply the renames one at a time, keeping tests green."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:3","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"function calculateDamageReimbursement(damage: Damage, item: Item): number {\n  if (effectiveEnchantment(item) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD) {\n    return damage.amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  return damage.amount;\n}"